Two entries match the same substring, spelled with different case. This is
`duplicate-match.toml`'s case one step sideways, and it is a separate fixture
because the two were separate behaviours until 2026-09-12: matching folds case,
so `Shared` and `shared` are ONE match, and the detector compared raw bytes and
saw two.

That gap was opened by the fold itself. The ruling that made `serves`
case-insensitive taught the MATCHER that two spellings are one match; the
DETECTOR that refuses two entries from claiming one match was not told, so a
document could declare `beta` and have it be unreachable for every input --
exactly the state `duplicate-match.toml` exists to refuse, wearing a different
spelling. A fresh instance found it by lesioning the fold and asking what else
the fold had made true.

The pair travels together: fold in one and you fold in the other, or the
invariant is a sentence rather than a rule.
